#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c34bce13-5054-5da9-8794-c9c152969ab8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3956" :question/problem "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1487/Source/Images/712a30860e04ee8c0d2a7a7bee06e0ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n- There is a zero at $x=0$.\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty, 0)$ and positive over $(0, \\infty)$.\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n- $y\\to-\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from above) as $x\\to-\\infty$.\n- $y\\to\\dfrac \\pi 2$ (from below) as $x\\to\\infty$.\n- The lines $y=\\pm\\dfrac \\pi 2$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is IV."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1587e39e-c7bf-5385-b726-a1b497f7c772" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b5293390-69dc-54d5-bfde-3580a0d5cdc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3957" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $\\dfrac \\pi 4$\n3. $-\\dfrac \\pi 3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1487/Source/Images/391a66b2aa1ff7fc7e80024a0dc2a91e.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny\\in\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac \\pi 3\n$$\n\nare in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nis *not* in the range of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nSo the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/id #uuid "be456bc5-fbcc-5b04-a2d4-efdd7223aefd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3958" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $2\\pi$\n2. $-1$\n3. $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1487/Source/Images/c23976dc6de719228dd2b13fa949687d.png)\n\nNote that the function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/id #uuid "74e3884e-e32f-553a-b832-b71fdc6a9172" :question/math-academy-id "e-3960" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arctan x$?\n\n1. $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0, \\pi\\right)$\n3. $\\left(0,0\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arctan x$, which is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\sqrt 3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\tan x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3,\\sqrt 3\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$.\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,\\pi\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\pi,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\tan x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arctan x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and $y=\\pi$ does not lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\pi, 0\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$.\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\tan x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arctan x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$, and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\arctan x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c21b6f60-3712-518e-9853-e56afb9aa36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/5acaaf6c4825f67fd5e4acb95b3d85068e9a030de4502d8baa5dd1dcd7888ea9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268783} {:answer/id #uuid "46f14eef-7d6d-52f6-933c-caa1682b0e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268784} {:answer/id #uuid "7b492756-cb1c-5b91-848c-c2ebf960810e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/