 :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268842} {:answer/id #uuid "b0658aa8-64b3-56c3-9715-b62e26aad75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63613311-48b0-51bd-b526-724ea67a5f42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no minimum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268839} :answer-field/id #uuid "72031391-21b0-59a6-99ea-8470e7e5cd21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46d16873-d982-5640-8e96-65da006b9174" :question/math-academy-id "q-42442" :question/problem "The minimum value of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/5cd9f04d8efcb74b08c4e2085011073b018e74ff2e57cbd7d7a69f261e2945b2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$ It never reaches the value $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nHence, there is no minimum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008e493f-7aa4-5d09-ad9a-df6dbd681756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/901cc06ce7661348fa18bb172f0a014e0c0d49e6f0a788cfe5926faa6e3061b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268846} {:answer/id #uuid "8bada9d1-98ca-53a4-afc7-7ec685965ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/b2939d0005167bb5771d7a566af50eff3c0d595ff95666d97d0d52597518f814.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268847} {:answer/id #uuid "54818c93-111e-5677-a7bd-4a3b571bee11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/7aadddedfe33accf816c81268b8873d02a324034e6ab26bdbfa9895989e7e63e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268848} {:answer/id #uuid "b8748eb1-3668-5945-add1-79c33ea74f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/e7aff749f5804fb774a334918b62ff9f20fd8e384fcebbc372e811ce51729d4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268849} {:answer/id #uuid "4e23f243-f662-5d5e-ae4a-f9e307090dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e23f243-f662-5d5e-ae4a-f9e307090dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268850} :answer-field/id #uuid "323399be-79c5-5681-88b5-f486f86a57be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e78d2563-d22e-5b86-b89f-f6aab65d4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43659" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93898618-2e85-5391-918c-ca99dd6b6d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109830} {:answer/id #uuid "c90db554-3297-5dc1-9349-04a7c1576cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109831} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c724a4-5aeb-599c-8f15-21dd10154cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109832} {:answer/id #uuid "e398e6fa-edb1-5e8f-812e-cd3f2d412b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109833} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb71f6b-1727-5dcf-927f-db07883efe6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e398e6fa-edb1-5e8f-812e-cd3f2d412b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109833} :answer-field/id #uuid "7734d521-f2a8-5ded-916d-0abc51466ff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00b3cd73-63d0-506c-b47a-e48c2b7ba801" :question/math-academy-id "q-65830" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arctan x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{4}, 1)$\n2. $(1, \\frac{π}{4})$\n3. $(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{4π}{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0c4e0c0-0c8c-5ce7-b1ae-37a48c88aa08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853} {:answer/id #uuid "f165483b-b658-5a50-adc8-d333f991d806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268854} {:answer/id #uuid "75a61cc9-e182-5af0-9a7d-bef61cbf2e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268855} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe47129-1269-550d-8ee5-2bdb26d1a369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268856} {:answer/id #uuid "4e465682-6d57-5ceb-a989-175913b5213d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f165483b-b658-5a50-adc8-d333f991d806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268854} :answer-field/id #uuid "d65f976a-9a55-56d0-8d01-c163185aff72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84a20de8-bfb9-5771-87ce-4b0eb965dc43" :question/math-academy-id "q-65979" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040c344e-d37a-5421-a555-f62c67aa0343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268860} {:answer/id #uuid "c81354cf-bbf2-5438-989f-32c0b0c737c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268861} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdec069-17cf-5c8a-8919-d967b715eb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268862} {:answer/id #uuid "7099c591-d7b1-5c3a-bd28-9be160343555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268863} {:answer/id #uuid "24d64247-0205-543f-a0e2-490f1d6b3427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7099c591-d7b1-5c3a-bd28-9be160343555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268863} :answer-field/id #uuid "49936ff6-5563-5dcd-88e6-ff051bc9ea7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e3f96a02-1b92-56b3-a674-52e950d6d8b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65991" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46e1513d-0944-5c09-8a7e-e1d9ae81bae5" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b53fec-fe3d-533f-9b36-09461f42bb01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/e5dab1e21f7d7a1e45676a7c360c9a9701b9dd3895d540a7a4a486047ced5361.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268867} {:answer/id #uuid "061e70bd-1a4a-5dec-b408-7e49c397f85b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/d9ac7238bd5a9be8b93269e6ed3d8ac540b472147e18dc12bbe5a57d80a22253.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268868} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c05ed7-7516-5c64-ab9f-4009c0a671ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13963369/assets/9ddfab27460f75bae2ece77fb623cbec0ccbaae61365dfa7e341e66306d6efb4.p